## Solution

**Setting up the modular condition.** We have $169 = 13^2$ and $\gcd(3,169)=1$, so $r_n = 3^{S_n}$ (where $S_n = r_{n-1}+r_{n-2}+r_{n-3}$) satisfies $r_n \equiv 1 \pmod{169}$ iff $S_n \equiv 0 \pmod{\operatorname{ord}_{169}(3)}$.

**Order of 3 modulo 169.** Modulo 13: $3^1\equiv3$, $3^2\equiv9$, $3^3\equiv 27\equiv 1\pmod{13}$, so $\operatorname{ord}_{13}(3)=3$. Since $3^3 = 27 = 1+2\cdot 13$ has $v_{13}(3^3-1)=1$, by the standard lifting lemma the order modulo $13^2$ is $3\cdot 13 = 39$ (and indeed $27\not\equiv 1\pmod{169}$). Thus
$$r_n \equiv 1\pmod{169} \iff S_n \equiv 0 \pmod{39}.$$

**Tracking $r_k \pmod{39}$.** For $k\ge 3$, $r_k = 3^{S_k}$ with $S_k\ge 3$, so $r_k\equiv 0\pmod 3$. Modulo 13, $r_k \equiv 3^{S_k \bmod 3}\pmod{13}$ (since $\operatorname{ord}_{13}(3)=3$). By CRT ($39=3\cdot 13$), for $k\ge 3$ the value $r_k \pmod{39}$ is determined by $a_k := S_k \bmod 3$:

| $a_k$ | $r_k\pmod{13}$ | $r_k\pmod{39}$ |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 1 | 27 |
| 1 | 3 | 3 |
| 2 | 9 | 9 |

(For $k=0,1,2$: $r_k=1\equiv 1\pmod{39}$.)

**Computing $a_k = (r_{k-1}+r_{k-2}+r_{k-3})\bmod 3$.** Since $r_k\equiv 0\pmod 3$ for $k\ge 3$ and $r_0=r_1=r_2\equiv 1\pmod 3$:

- $a_3 = (1+1+1)\bmod 3 = 0 \Rightarrow r_3\equiv 27\pmod{39}$.
- $a_4 = (1+1+0)\bmod 3 = 2 \Rightarrow r_4\equiv 9\pmod{39}$.
- $a_5 = (1+0+0)\bmod 3 = 1 \Rightarrow r_5\equiv 3\pmod{39}$.
- $a_6 = (0+0+0)\bmod 3 = 0 \Rightarrow r_6\equiv 27\pmod{39}$.
- For all $k\ge 6$: all three predecessors have index $\ge 3$, so $a_k=0$, giving $r_k\equiv 27\pmod{39}$.

**Evaluating $S_n \pmod{39}$ for small $n$:**

- $n=3$: $S_3 = 1+1+1 = 3 \not\equiv 0\pmod{39}$.
- $n=4$: $S_4 = 1+1+r_3 \equiv 1+1+27 = 29 \not\equiv 0\pmod{39}$.
- $n=5$: $S_5 = 1+r_3+r_4 \equiv 1+27+9 = 37 \not\equiv 0\pmod{39}$.
- $n=6$: $S_6 = r_3+r_4+r_5 \equiv 27+9+3 = 39 \equiv 0\pmod{39}$. ✓

**For $n\ge 9$** (so all three terms have index $\ge 6$): $S_n \equiv 27+27+27 = 81 \equiv 3\pmod{39}\ne 0$, so no $n\ge 9$ works. (One may check $n=7$: $S_7\equiv 9+3+27=39\equiv0$, and $n=8$: $S_8\equiv 3+27+27=57\equiv 18\ne 0$; but $6<7$.)

**Conclusion.** The least $n$ with $S_n\equiv 0\pmod{39}$, hence $r_n\equiv 1\pmod{169}$, is $n=6$.

$$\boxed{n = 6}$$

